  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    EU buyers using the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> now have a new customs cost to model.
    From <strong>1 July 2026</strong>, EU guidance says a temporary <strong>€3 customs duty per item</strong> applies to low-value distance-sale imports up to €150, replacing the old duty-free treatment for those parcels until the EU e-commerce data hub phase.
    This article translates that change into spreadsheet-haul planning, not legal advice.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed on 1 July 2026?</h2>
  <p>
    The European Commission’s June 2026 guidance says the EU will apply a temporary flat customs duty of <strong>€3 per item</strong> on low-value consignments imported from outside the EU.
    The official notice frames the measure as part of the transition toward fuller e-commerce customs reform and product traceability.
    You can read the EU notice here:
    <a href="https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/news/guidance-and-legal-text-temporary-flat-fee-low-value-imports-which-will-apply-until-1-july-2028-2026-06-08_en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU Taxation and Customs Union guidance</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why “per item” matters for mixed hauls</h2>
  <p>
    For Kakobuy shoppers, the practical question is not “is my parcel under €150?” but “how many distinct customs items are inside the parcel?”
    A simple T-shirt-only parcel and a mixed parcel containing shoes, perfume, electronics, and accessories can behave differently because item classification drives the duty logic.
    That is why the old habit of asking “what is the cheapest declaration?” is the wrong frame.
    The better frame is: what are the contents, how many categories are combined, and does consolidation still make sense after customs friction?
  </p>

